      // [src_jemison_nasabio, src_jemison_nwhm, src_jemison_nbc_starship, src_jemison_nbc_discovery | 1979-2012 | NASA biography + museum biography + NBC News] NASA's biography lists a grant for health studies in rural Kenya (1979) and work in a refugee camp in Thailand (1980); the museum biography says she applied to NASA in 1985, reapplied after the Challenger accident paused selection and was chosen in 1987 as one of 15 from over 2,000 applicants, and that after leaving NASA she launched an international science camp and founded a technology consulting company; NBC News reports that she founded several ventures including the Jemison Group and the Dorothy Jemison Foundation for Excellence and that the foundation she headed led the team awarded DARPA's $500,000 100 Year Starship seed contract. Repeated self-started opportunities from student years to 2012, but the early items are one-line entries in NASA's own list and a biography built partly on her memoir, and the later ones are press reports of the ventures rather than accounts of how she ran them, so strong inference at a moderate confidence.
      proactive_agency: [68, 0.54, "s", "A"],
      // [src_jemison_status, src_jemison_presskit, src_jemison_nbc_starship | 1992, 2012 | NASA mission status reports + STS-47 press kit + NBC News] The status reports show the ground frog-embryology team agreeing with her choice of the two best egg-donor frogs of the four aboard, the payload manager approving a plan by Jemison and Jan Davis to free a stuck workstation door, and a Japanese co-investigator thanking her for her hard work on the Liquid Drop Facility experiment although its results were not completely successful; the press kit describes the paired 12-hour Blue and Red Team shifts built around ground investigators; NBC News quotes a partner organisation's director saying each of the three groups in her 100 Year Starship team brought a different emphasis, hers being education and broader social goals. Documented working partnerships with investigators and partners, but Spacelab flight work is collaborative by design and the 2012 item is a partner's description of a newly formed team, so strong inference at a moderate confidence and a modest score.
